From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:40:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: mcc: add a T8132 switch to replay macOS's
 controller setup

Traced under the hypervisor from its first instruction, macOS's kernel
writes the T8132 memory controllers within 0.53 s of starting: each
AMCC plane's enable word built from 0 one bit at a time, each after a
configuration word, then the stage words with their range words, and
the lane tables of the DCS channel pages. iBoot leaves them at 0, and
Linux writes none of them.

On the M4 MacBook Pro a 512 MiB pointer chase reads 98-99 ns under
Linux on every kernel tried. Against macOS's walk after a light load
(100.3 ns) that is parity, but after a memory burst macOS reads 91.3 ns
and Linux does not change. The DCS and fabric votes are already at
their top states and moving them changes nothing, so the controller
setup Linux never writes is what is left to test.

Add the 119 of those writes that lie inside the controller register
ranges this driver maps, in macOS's order, and a test switch,
apple_mcc.t8132_entry_replay, that writes them at probe before cache
allocation is enabled. Nothing outside those ranges is mapped, read or
written; the DCS channel pages and the region-lock words are left out.
The default, N, writes nothing.

Assisted-by: LLM
